n=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6293\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6331\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6664\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6700\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2327\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2{\\mu }_{1}-3{\\mu }_{2},4{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+9{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(4-3,28+36) \\\\  & ∼N(1,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-4.6<Y<3.48).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-4.6<Y<3.48) & =P(\\frac{-4.6-1}{8}<Z<\\frac{3.48-1}{8}) \\\\  & =P(-0.70<Z<0.31) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.31)-P(Z<-0.70) \\\\  & =Φ(0.31)-Φ(-0.70)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.31) & ≈0.6217 \\\\ Φ(-0.70) & ≈0.2420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-4.6<Y<3.48) & ≈Φ(0.31)-Φ(-0.70) \\\\  & ≈0.6217-0.2420 \\\\  & ≈0.3797.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158942 :answer-field/correct 17592186158943 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f962dd14-3b46-5fda-bac4-2a4b37326612" :question/id #uuid "f962dd14-3b46-5fda-bac4-2a4b37326612" :question/math-academy-id "q-109671" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(8,{40}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(30,{30}^{2})$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8315\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8340\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8531\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8577\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8749\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8770\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8790\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(8-30,{40}^{2}+{30}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-22,2500).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-22$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{2500}=50.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =P(\\frac{-31-(-22)}{50}\\le Z\\le \\frac{31-(-22)}{50}) \\\\  & =P(-0.18\\le Z\\le 1.06) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.06)-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.18) & ≈0.4286 \\\\ Φ(1.06) & ≈0.8554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.8554-0.4286 \\\\  & ≈0.43.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6397adad-43cc-59f3-a17a-dddd5e5332c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6397adad-43cc-59f3-a17a-dddd5e5332c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "6397adad-43cc-59f3-a17a-dddd5e5332c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-063e914b-6ae9-5f1f-9506-be4bc66385c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f6bb991-2e99-5c99-ab85-9ebe58e246e0" "answer-c8871607-886d-50a9-b06f-bece754672c1" "answer-0be4d607-07b8-5a53-9ef5-3fec0375c06b" "answer-ca2030c2-fd4a-5c57-9be2-034d8e3da1e7" "answer-063e914b-6ae9-5f1f-9506-be4bc66385c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f6bb991-2e99-5c99-ab85-9ebe58e246e0" :answer/id #uuid "3f6bb991-2e99-5c99-ab85-9ebe58e246e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8871607-886d-50a9-b06f-bece754672c1" :answer/id #uuid "c8871607-886d-50a9-b06f-bece754672c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0be4d607-07b8-5a53-9ef5-3fec0375c06b" :answer/id #uuid "0be4d607-07b8-5a53-9ef5-3fec0375c06b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca2030c2-fd4a-5c57-9be2-034d8e3da1e7" :answer/id #uuid "ca2030c2-fd4a-5c57-9be2-034d8e3da1e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-063e914b-6ae9-5f1f-9506-be4bc66385c9" :answer/id #uuid "063e914b-6ae9-5f1f-9506-be4bc66385c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e624f6f-227d-5bbb-b9ec-897830d915af" :question/id #uuid "7e624f6f-227d-5bbb-b9ec-897830d915af" :question/math-academy-id "q-109668" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(1,0.4)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(4,6)$ are independent and $Y=5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<13.4).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 1+4,25\\cdot 0.4+6) \\\\  & ∼N(9,16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =9$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<13.4).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<13.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.4-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.1) \\\\  & =Φ(1.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.1)≈0.8643.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<13.4)≈0.86.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c622c6db-0cfb-5314-962b-7b684cde7e09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c622c6db-0cfb-5314-962b-7b684cde7e09" :answer-field/id #uuid "c622c6db-0cfb-5314-962b-7b684cde7e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51759a7d-87c9-562a-8312-f8b6d8fc5142" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59500d77-cde2-5c82-b94d-9272e1f58b92" "answer-51759a7d-87c9-562a-8312-f8b6d8fc5142" "answer-8b96a22a-c2c5-5cc0-ac69-7def23646d93" "answer-00c0bdf1-ca0d-5a57-8521-96c07af1afa4" "answer-95e8df01-f50d-5ef6-8b62-b148b070af96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59500d77-cde2-5c82-b94d-9272e1f58b92" :answer/id #uuid "59500d77-cde2-5c82-b94d-9272e1f58b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51759a7d-87c9-562a-8312-f8b6d8fc5142" :answer/id #uuid "51759a7d-87c9-562a-8312-f8b6d8fc5142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.86" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b96a22a-c2c5-5cc0-ac69-7def23646d93" :answer/id #uuid "8b96a22a-c2c5-5cc0-ac69-7def23646d93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00c0bdf1-ca0d-5a57-8521-96c07af1afa4" :answer/id #uuid "00c0bdf1-ca0d-5a57-8521-96c07af1afa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e8df01-f50d-5ef6-8b62-b148b070af96" :answer/id #uuid "95e8df01-f50d-5ef6-8b62-b148b070af96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6718d3e2-fbcd-51c5-808c-744242ca4d81" :question/id #uuid "6718d3e2-fbcd-51c5-808c-744242ca4d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109754" :question/problem "A Japanese-style table tennis racket is made from a wooden blade covered with rubber on one side.  The weight of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $80\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $31\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weight of the rubber is normally distributed with a mean of $45\\,\\text{g}$ grams and a variance of $50\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ If one racket is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $128\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of the blade, and let $Y$ denote the weight of the rubber. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>128).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(80,31)$ and $Y∼N(45,50),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(80+45,31+50) \\\\  & ∼N(125,81).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =125$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>128),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & =P(Z>\\frac{128-125}{9}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.33) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & ≈1-Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈1-0.6293 \\\\  & ≈0.3707.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-23eb8fea-b998-548f-a61c-cbabe9a91a03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23eb8fea-b998-548f-a61c-cbabe9a91a03" :answer-field/id #uuid "23eb8fea-b998-548f-a61c-cbabe9a91a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09de9872-6ab1-5e3a-a36a-ad0d1711044d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09de9872-6ab1-5e3a-a36a-ad0d1711044d" "answer-d0a109a1-7fb3-53b4-9819-0e61d9c1e053" "answer-3a56d3b4-a632-5cfa-b0ad-7cecce636e2f" "answer-785ba3d5-a85c-5689-b7d9-75e025455242" "answer-a2e85dc2-1f34-5360-b990-3d51b751b711"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09de9872-6ab1-5e3a-a36a-ad0d1711044d" :answer/id #uuid "09de9872-6ab1-5e3a-a36a-ad0d1711044d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3707" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0a109a1-7fb3-53b4-9819-0e61d9c1e053" :answer/id #uuid "d0a109a1-7fb3-53b4-9819-0e61d9c1e053" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3783" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a56d3b4-a632-5cfa-b0ad-7cecce636e2f" :answer/id #uuid "3a56d3b4-a632-5cfa-b0ad-7cecce636e2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4129" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-785ba3d5-a85c-5689-b7d9-75e025455242" :answer/id #uuid "785ba3d5-a85c-5689-b7d9-75e025455242" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4207" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2e85dc2-1f34-5360-b990-3d51b751b711" :answer/id #uuid "a2e85dc2-1f34-5360-b990-3d51b751b711" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4522" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7f40d31-3891-595e-9dc0-80e5713161b9" :question/id #uuid "e7f40d31-3891-595e-9dc0-80e5713161b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109757" :question/problem "A particular shop sells chickens and turkeys. The weight of a chicken is normally distributed with a mean of $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$ The weight of a turkey is normally distributed with a mean of $8\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $0.4\\,\\text{kg}.$ If a chicken and a turkey are randomly selected, what is the probability that $5$ times the weight of the chicken is greater than the turkey's weight? You may assume that the weights are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ | $\\,0.8133\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ | $\\,0.8365\\,$ | $\\,0.8389\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chicken, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a turkey. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(5X>Y)=P(5X-Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(1.5,{0.1}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(8,{0.4}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5X-Y & ∼N(5\\cdot 1.5-8,{5}^{2}\\cdot {0.1}^{2}+{0.4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-0.5,0.41).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $5X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-0.5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.41}.$\n\nTo find $P(5X-Y>0),$ we convert $5X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-(-0.5)}{\\sqrt{0.41}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.78) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.78)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.78)≈0.7823.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.78) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7823 \\\\  & 0.2177.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76f631c0-460f-51ec-9363-bfa37aabf5c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76f631c0-460f-51ec-9363-bfa37aabf5c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "76f631c0-460f-51ec-9363-bfa37aabf5c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3beb759e-3832-5b91-8bb0-5fe04a4c0eb3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72ce0fe1-3792-56d6-8c38-71a07f2ff7ca" "answer-cee6069b-3ea8-5bd5-9b1b-39d0ed56f8fe" "answer-0b27ab74-19c6-52de-9b88-0b5d2b3023d3" "answer-3beb759e-3832-5b91-8bb0-5fe04a4c0eb3" "answer-a683289d-1445-55db-90cf-1e6eff679e06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72ce0fe1-3792-56d6-8c38-71a07f2ff7ca" :answer/id #uuid "72ce0fe1-3792-56d6-8c38-71a07f2ff7ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1660" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cee6069b-3ea8-5bd5-9b1b-39d0ed56f8fe" :answer/id #uuid "cee6069b-3ea8-5bd5-9b1b-39d0ed56f8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1867" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b27ab74-19c6-52de-9b88-0b5d2b3023d3" :answer/id #uuid "0b27ab74-19c6-52de-9b88-0b5d2b3023d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1949" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3beb759e-3832-5b91-8bb0-5fe04a4c0eb3" :answer/id #uuid "3beb759e-3832-5b91-8bb0-5fe04a4c0eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2177" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a683289d-1445-55db-90cf-1e6eff679e06" :answer/id #uuid "a683289d-1445-55db-90cf-1e6eff679e06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2266" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb6e266d-fd9f-5c8c-82b7-fde74e3fe145" :question/id #uuid "bb6e266d-fd9f-5c8c-82b7-fde74e3fe145" :question/math-academy-id "q-175476" :question/problem "A particular shop sells watermelons and melons. The weight of a watermelon is normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{kg}.$ The weight of a melon is normally distributed with a mean of $5\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{kg}.$ If one watermelon and one melon are randomly selected, what is the probability that the weight of the watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of the melon? You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ | $\\,0.7549\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a watermelon, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a melon. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X>3Y)=P(X-3Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(5,{2}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-3Y & ∼N(10-3\\cdot 5,16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,52).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-3Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{52}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-3Y>0),$ we convert $X-3Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0+5}{\\sqrt{52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.69)≈0.7549.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.69) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7549 \\\\  & ≈0.2451.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the weight of a watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of a melon is $0.2451.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd9b0e70-58cf-5f85-82e2-913249f58302"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd9b0e70-58cf-5f85-82e2-913249f58302" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9b0e70-58cf-5f85-82e2-913249f58302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbdd52b6-d064-5b8a-87cc-1efdc1299079" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff64d49e-8bb7-595f-8e20-451c46ffaab2" "answer-fbdd52b6-d064-5b8a-87cc-1efdc1299079" "answer-f0de7d0f-e915-5b9a-9bba-67dc042d08bc" "answer-1709490b-7f5e-5a60-9c40-0d9cf3cceda8" "answer-6b31c841-227b-52d0-915b-6cf71392b90f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff64d49e-8bb7-595f-8e20-451c46ffaab2" :answer/id #uuid "ff64d49e-8bb7-595f-8e20-451c46ffaab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2206" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbdd52b6-d064-5b8a-87cc-1efdc1299079" :answer/id #uuid "fbdd52b6-d064-5b8a-87cc-1efdc1299079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2451" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0de7d0f-e915-5b9a-9bba-67dc042d08bc" :answer/id #uuid "f0de7d0f-e915-5b9a-9bba-67dc042d08bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2514" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1709490b-7f5e-5a60-9c40-0d9cf3cceda8" :answer/id #uuid "1709490b-7f5e-5a60-9c40-0d9cf3cceda8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2776" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b31c841-227b-52d0-915b-6cf71392b90f" :answer/id #uuid "6b31c841-227b-52d0-915b-6cf71392b90f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2843" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c9343e5-40f1-5766-8346-83637c7ee445" :question/id #uuid "0c9343e5-40f1-5766-8346-83637c7ee445" :question/math-academy-id "q-109759" :question/problem "In a technical support call center, waiting times are normally distributed with a mean of $15$ minutes and a standard deviation of $3$ minutes. The conversation times are normally distributed with a mean of $10$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. The time to solve a problem is the sum of the waiting time and the conversation time. What is the probability that it took more than $30$ minutes to solve the problem of a randomly selected caller? You may assume that waiting and conversation times are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the waiting time, and let $Y$ denote the conversation time. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>30).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(15,{3}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(10,{4}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(15+10,{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(25,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =25$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>30),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-25}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)≈0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8413 \\\\  & ≈0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b619dfd9-b2dd-59c0-870d-da366a920039"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b619dfd9-b2dd-59c0-870d-da366a920039" :answer-field/id #uuid "b619dfd9-b2dd-59c0-870d-da366a920039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bee4b9cc-203d-5c1c-bc20-21d933ffd030" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24120408-2087-55b0-b11d-72781143b0e9" "answer-85c254a0-62c2-59a8-886c-22c3f672d045" "answer-9eff9744-fffc-5560-b024-8d235dec8231" "answer-a4b71db8-fce7-543b-b560-c183dd798dc6" "answer-bee4b9cc-203d-5c1c-bc20-21d933ffd030"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24120408-2087-55b0-b11d-72781143b0e9" :answer/id #uuid "24120408-2087-55b0-b11d-72781143b0e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1093" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85c254a0-62c2-59a8-886c-22c3f672d045" :answer/id #uuid "85c254a0-62c2-59a8-886c-22c3f672d045" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9eff9744-fffc-5560-b024-8d235dec8231" :answer/id #uuid "9eff9744-fffc-5560-b024-8d235dec8231" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1335" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4b71db8-fce7-543b-b560-c183dd798dc6" :answer/id #uuid "a4b71db8-fce7-543b-b560-c183dd798dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1357" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bee4b9cc-203d-5c1c-bc20-21d933ffd030" :answer/id #uuid "bee4b9cc-203d-5c1c-bc20-21d933ffd030" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1587" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162297 :question/problem "A shop sells spray foam in small and large cans. The volume of liquid in a small can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.3\\,\\text{ltrs}$ and a standard deviation of $0.003\\,\\text{ltrs}.$ The volume of liquid in a large can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.6\\,\\text{ltrs}$ and a standard deviation of $0.008\\,\\text{ltrs}.$\n\nThe foam from a small can expands $40$ times its liquid volume after being sprayed in place, while the foam from a large can expands to $60$ times its liquid volume. \nIf one small and one large can are randomly selected, what is the probability that the total foam volume from both cans after expanding is less than $49\\,\\text{ltrs}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the volume of liquid in a small can, and let $Y$ denote the volume of liquid in a big can. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(40X+60Y<49).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(0.3,{0.003}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(0.6,{0.008}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40X+60Y & ∼N(40\\cdot 0.3+60\\cdot 0.6,{40}^{2}\\cdot {0.003}^{2}+{60}^{2}\\cdot {0.008}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(48,0.2448).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $40X+60Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =48$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.2448}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(40X+60Y<49),$ we convert $40X+60Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(40X+60Y<49) & =P(Z<\\frac{49-48}{\\sqrt{0.2448}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<2.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.02) \\\\  & =Φ(2.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.02)≈0.9783.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(40X+60Y<49) & ≈Φ(2.02) \\\\  & ≈0.9783.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that, after expanding, the total volume of foam is less than $49$ liters is $0.9783.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158984 :answer-field/correct 17592186158986 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163271 :tutorial/content "Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be *independent* random variables that are normally distributed with\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe can show that the random variable $Y = X_1\\pm X_2$ is normally distributed, and\n\n$$\nY =  X_1\\pm X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_1\\pm\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(2+1,3+4)∼N(3,7),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(2-1,3+4)∼N(1,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen computing a sum or difference of normal random variables, there are a few things to watch out for:\n\n- Firstly, when we compute the distribution of a *difference*, we subtract the means, but *add* the variances!\n\n- Secondly, it is vital to check that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent. Cases where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *dependent* will be dealt with in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163285 :tutorial/content "If $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are *independent* random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4+3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(7,48),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4-3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(1,48).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162166 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a" :question/id #uuid "2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163499" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $6\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9a/9ac139d8823f326f32869f308cb2d04cdc9e0d86dba7d4a11b66955afd665da3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =k(\\frac{(0)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(0)^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=6\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =k(\\frac{(6)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(6)^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ 0 & =k(324-360)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-36k+6 \\\\ 36k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a78871b-3da5-52c1-a57c-cb87914fafa1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a78871b-3da5-52c1-a57c-cb87914fafa1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78871b-3da5-52c1-a57c-cb87914fafa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8690093-4909-568c-bd3a-20b77037a0fe" "answer-2c3d3836-b51f-5e7c-a7a9-704639b3e9bf" "answer-c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" "answer-528dca58-912a-5c4e-81e7-257ba6b0db7e" "answer-6b4182f5-2803-552b-853e-c778c967ac69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8690093-4909-568c-bd3a-20b77037a0fe" :answer/id #uuid "c8690093-4909-568c-bd3a-20b77037a0fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c3d3836-b51f-5e7c-a7a9-704639b3e9bf" :answer/id #uuid "2c3d3836-b51f-5e7c-a7a9-704639b3e9bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" :answer/id #uuid "c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-528dca58-912a-5c4e-81e7-257ba6b0db7e" :answer/id #uuid "528dca58-912a-5c4e-81e7-257ba6b0db7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b4182f5-2803-552b-853e-c778c967ac69" :answer/id #uuid "6b4182f5-2803-552b-853e-c778c967ac69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3aa3a140-5e95-5af8-9fd5-684dff535064" "question-8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3aa3a140-5e95-5af8-9fd5-684dff535064" :question/id #uuid "3aa3a140-5e95-5af8-9fd5-684dff535064" :question/math-academy-id "q-163561" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/602ef5480fe59ccf921d17a19d10848a1a8a04a1fc3fb3181b8e9b17ebe81fad.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(7-x) & =0 \\\\ 7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{10})^{2} & =k(7(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 20 & =12k+8 \\\\ 12k & =12 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+16}=\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-096d2af6-6379-5a37-9ed1-9857796a70e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-096d2af6-6379-5a37-9ed1-9857796a70e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "096d2af6-6379-5a37-9ed1-9857796a70e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45fc70ca-1697-5db6-b1f1-c08ca6e6db5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f39b3f3-f271-59a4-bf86-3f809a0727f9" "answer-f55ad6c7-9522-5c97-be01-f1f507abc917" "answer-b2128b20-1288-5387-bc4f-30b80ffe9c21" "answer-6b84233d-39b9-5907-bb7e-b896ae67e3f1" "answer-45fc70ca-1697-5db6-b1f1-c08ca6e6db5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f39b3f3-f271-59a4-bf86-3f809a0727f9" :answer/id #uuid "1f39b3f3-f271-59a4-bf86-3f809a0727f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f55ad6c7-9522-5c97-be01-f1f507abc917" :answer/id #uuid "f55ad6c7-9522-5c97-be01-f1f507abc917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2128b20-1288-5387-bc4f-30b80ffe9c21" :answer/id #uuid "b2128b20-1288-5387-bc4f-30b80ffe9c21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b84233d-39b9-5907-bb7e-b896ae67e3f1" :answer/id #uuid "6b84233d-39b9-5907-bb7e-b896ae67e3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{53}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45fc70ca-1697-5db6-b1f1-c08ca6e6db5c" :answer/id #uuid "45fc70ca-1697-5db6-b1f1-c08ca6e6db5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536" :question/id #uuid "8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536" :question/math-academy-id "q-163566" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73da2be2514373e6d0b1052f0eca21c339b4d826d38621faf72b3e251d7d8a95.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(4-2x) & =0 \\\\ 4-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(4-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(4(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =k(4(1)-{(1)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 32 & =3k+8 \\\\ 3k & =24 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =32x-8{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-16{x}^{2}+64x+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=4\\sqrt{-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1}=4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10849e2a-d2b8-5eda-a82b-458c2096cbcd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10849e2a-d2b8-5eda-a82b-458c2096cbcd" :answer-field/id #uuid "10849e2a-d2b8-5eda-a82b-458c2096cbcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" :answer-field/choices ["answer-382ad457-c78c-5db2-b511-049b1e51f672" "answer-02c1d6a8-802b-5aac-9cd8-94b55465e8d3" "answer-dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" "answer-95d62877-62ed-576b-b8c1-68b72d5fa0f1" "answer-f81daac3-e6be-5bee-9268-19f1a228f0ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-382ad457-c78c-5db2-b511-049b1e51f672" :answer/id #uuid "382ad457-c78c-5db2-b511-049b1e51f672" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02c1d6a8-802b-5aac-9cd8-94b55465e8d3" :answer/id #uuid "02c1d6a8-802b-5aac-9cd8-94b55465e8d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" :answer/id #uuid "dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95d62877-62ed-576b-b8c1-68b72d5fa0f1" :answer/id #uuid "95d62877-62ed-576b-b8c1-68b72d5fa0f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f81daac3-e6be-5bee-9268-19f1a228f0ae" :answer/id #uuid "f81daac3-e6be-5bee-9268-19f1a228f0ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a2048220-14aa-5206-bbbd-f88860b43a2a" :activity/id #uuid "a2048220-14aa-5206-bbbd-f88860b43a2a" :activity/title "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048388 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-6298506a-6ab4-5316-a7ce-7e5bfd41dc85" "step-c9baaed7-d44a-530e-b75a-7fd6fd6eb3d1" "step-fc3bb8d9-68c2-5b90-9003-ab8666f160bc" "step-2f006507-cb5e-5231-8769-1e44e53b48f6" "step-9d41f50b-d402-55a6-b472-184dbf89c69d"] :activity/first-step "step-6298506a-6ab4-5316-a7ce-7e5bfd41dc85" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6298506a-6ab4-5316-a7ce-7e5bfd41dc85" :step/id #uuid "6298506a-6ab4-5316-a7ce-7e5bfd41dc85" :step/content "question-2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a" :step/next "step-c9baaed7-d44a-530e-b75a-7fd6fd6eb3d1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c9baaed7-d44a-530e-b75a-7fd6fd6eb3d1" :step/id #uuid "c9baaed7-d44a-530e-b75a-7fd6fd6eb3d1" :step/content 17592186365288 :step/next "step-fc3bb8d9-68c2-5b90-9003-ab8666f160bc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fc3bb8d9-68c2-5b90-9003-ab8666f160bc" :step/id #uuid "fc3bb8d9-68c2-5b90-9003-ab8666f160bc" :step/content "question-3aa3a140-5e95-5af8-9fd5-684dff535064" :step/next "step-2f006507-cb5e-5231-8769-1e44e53b48f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2f006507-cb5e-5231-8769-1e44e53b48f6" :step/id #uuid "2f006507-cb5e-5231-8769-1e44e53b48f6" :step/content 17592186365329 :step/next "step-9d41f50b-d402-55a6-b472-184dbf89c69d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9d41f50b-d402-55a6-b472-184dbf89c69d" :step/id #uuid "9d41f50b-d402-55a6-b472-184dbf89c69d" :step/content "question-8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f927f6ff-ccc6-56f9-848c-de38a72fd309" "question-5e4a27b0-0664-5456-aded-ea139acd3d27" "question-e4eb324e-31ac-540b-94ba-f7c7a69f257d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138310 :question/problem "The gravitational acceleration $g$ of a body in free fall is given by the formula\n\n$$\ng = \\dfrac{2h}{t^2},\n$$\n\n where $h$ is the distance, measured in meters, traveled by the body due to gravity in time $t,$ measured in seconds.\nDetermine an appropriate measurement unit for gravitational acceleration." :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of gravitational acceleration, we substitute the given units of distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g & =\\frac{2h}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}.$ Here, we ignore the unitless constant $2$ since it does not affect the units." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d7c30aa-a3e9-5b45-be5b-cfccc139072e" "question-eced41ae-3fab-5c67-a3bf-aedcd9f64978"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138311 :question/problem "The frequency of a wave is given by the formula\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{v}{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the wave in $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}},$ while $l$ represents the length of the wave in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for frequency." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of frequency by substituting the given units of velocity and length into the formula and simplifying. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f & =\\frac{v}{l} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{m}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of frequency is $\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{s}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-177c4027-65c0-5159-869d-c7c5edbf2c0b" "question-ff3d9c94-77b4-51bb-806f-e93681c391b2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138312 :question/problem "A farm with $115$ chickens produces $20\\,000$ eggs per year.  To determine the number of eggs $P$ produced by one chicken on average, the farmer uses the formula\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{N}{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of eggs produced by the farm during the year, and $C$ is the number of chickens on the farm. \n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $115$ and $20\\,000.$ They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $N$ represents $\\textrm{eggs}$ and $C$ represents $\\textrm{chickens},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $P=\\dfrac{N}{C}$ is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{eggs}}{\\textrm{chickens}}.$ In words, this is \"eggs per chicken.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f927f6ff-ccc6-56f9-848c-de38a72fd309" :question/id #uuid "f927f6ff-ccc6-56f9-848c-de38a72fd309" :question/math-academy-id "q-46081" :question/problem "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of work, we substitute the given units of force and distance into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =F\\cdot d \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c9253cc-52ac-5d1c-b3ee-ba5595fbaccc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c9253cc-52ac-5d1c-b3ee-ba5595fbaccc" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c9253cc-52ac-5d1c-b3ee-ba5595fbaccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb4b5f65-0be1-558d-93d2-208a32c43b71" "answer-b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" "answer-14976fe2-9b52-5ebe-8c73-8ff28cb9c14c" "answer-7c54496e-2150-55be-ad11-f0813a6820d3" "answer-c72b361b-647a-5fbe-98d9-e79fe7657b16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb4b5f65-0be1-558d-93d2-208a32c43b71" :answer/id #uuid "fb4b5f65-0be1-558d-93d2-208a32c43b71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" :answer/id #uuid "b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14976fe2-9b52-5ebe-8c73-8ff28cb9c14c" :answer/id #uuid "14976fe2-9b52-5ebe-8c73-8ff28cb9c14c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{s}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{kg}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c54496e-2150-55be-ad11-f0813a6820d3" :answer/id #uuid "7c54496e-2150-55be-ad11-f0813a6820d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c72b361b-647a-5fbe-98d9-e79fe7657b16" :answer/id #uuid "c72b361b-647a-5fbe-98d9-e79fe7657b16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e4a27b0-0664-5456-aded-ea139acd3d27" :question/id #uuid "5e4a27b0-0664-5456-aded-ea139acd3d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-46155" :question/problem "The work applied to an object is the result of multiplying the force by the distance. Given that the force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}),$ determine the unit of work in terms of kilograms, meters and seconds." :question/worked-solution "The force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and distance is measured in meters ($\\text{m}$). Since the work is the multiplication of force by distance, we must multiply force units by distance units. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50db8c22-bd5b-5673-ad8f-e5d45d8e0739"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50db8c22-bd5b-5673-ad8f-e5d45d8e0739" :answer-field/id #uuid "50db8c22-bd5b-5673-ad8f-e5d45d8e0739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" :answer-field/choices ["answer-defedfc3-6a0c-570a-8e21-65d713653ddd" "answer-4cfc7a0e-24fa-511e-8e2a-c033f6a57641" "answer-4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" "answer-daaa803f-6528-5c7c-ab17-6235345378e4" "answer-1d762ea4-afec-5d24-987b-1629fe8078d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-defedfc3-6a0c-570a-8e21-65d713653ddd" :answer/id #uuid "defedfc3-6a0c-570a-8e21-65d713653ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cfc7a0e-24fa-511e-8e2a-c033f6a57641" :answer/id #uuid "4cfc7a0e-24fa-511e-8e2a-c033f6a57641" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" :answer/id #uuid "4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daaa803f-6528-5c7c-ab17-6235345378e4" :answer/id #uuid "daaa803f-6528-5c7c-ab17-6235345378e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d762ea4-afec-5d24-987b-1629fe8078d3" :answer/id #uuid "1d762ea4-afec-5d24-987b-1629fe8078d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4eb324e-31ac-540b-94ba-f7c7a69f257d" :question/id #uuid "e4eb324e-31ac-540b-94ba-f7c7a69f257d" :question/math-academy-id "q-46150" :question/problem "The formula that relates the work $W$ applied to an object with mass $m$, distance $d$ and time $t$ is $W=\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}.$ What are the units of work in terms of base units?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of work, we substitute the base units of mass, distance, and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48b5e27f-972a-5f60-9f56-8e1482b67a34"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48b5e27f-972a-5f60-9f56-8e1482b67a34" :answer-field/id #uuid "48b5e27f-972a-5f60-9f56-8e1482b67a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" :answer-field/choices ["answer-927f33a2-7b1f-56c9-8fca-fb0a267ad22f" "answer-037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" "answer-6f34b9f2-d299-5888-b1a9-3477433b7d6e" "answer-40256bc8-0f04-5875-90c7-812b6705e3bc" "answer-1de930e2-59b8-55c2-9276-ff6f790b54aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-927f33a2-7b1f-56c9-8fca-fb0a267ad22f" :answer/id #uuid "927f33a2-7b1f-56c9-8fca-fb0a267ad22f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" :answer/id #uuid "037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f34b9f2-d299-5888-b1a9-3477433b7d6e" :answer/id #uuid "6f34b9f2-d299-5888-b1a9-3477433b7d6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{kg}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40256bc8-0f04-5875-90c7-812b6705e3bc" :answer/id #uuid "40256bc8-0f04-5875-90c7-812b6705e3bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1de930e2-59b8-55c2-9276-ff6f790b54aa" :answer/id #uuid "1de930e2-59b8-55c2-9276-ff6f790b54aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d7c30aa-a3e9-5b45-be5b-cfccc139072e" :question/id #uuid "9d7c30aa-a3e9-5b45-be5b-cfccc139072e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105397" :question/problem "The magnitude of a force $F$ applied to an object is related to the work $W$ and the distance $d$ by the formula $F=\\frac{W}{d}.$  Given that $W$ is measured in $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and the distance is measured in meters, determine an appropriate measurement unit for the force." :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f644e2e4-a4d8-546c-9e9b-b5558284b434"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f644e2e4-a4d8-546c-9e9b-b5558284b434" :answer-field/id #uuid "f644e2e4-a4d8-546c-9e9b-b5558284b434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4fce19de-60b6-585b-a388-1fa1eeb5a3ee" "answer-ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" "answer-894020f7-35dc-5ea3-907a-86bdf3be687c" "answer-79a01367-cc89-5f4a-869f-97de82fd3129" "answer-7f0d7771-34e6-5d10-a9a3-70e0207f524d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fce19de-60b6-585b-a388-1fa1eeb5a3ee" :answer/id #uuid "4fce19de-60b6-585b-a388-1fa1eeb5a3ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" :answer/id #uuid "ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-894020f7-35dc-5ea3-907a-86bdf3be687c" :answer/id #uuid "894020f7-35dc-5ea3-907a-86bdf3be687c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{m}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79a01367-cc89-5f4a-869f-97de82fd3129" :answer/id #uuid "79a01367-cc89-5f4a-869f-97de82fd3129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f0d7771-34e6-5d10-a9a3-70e0207f524d" :answer/id #uuid "7f0d7771-34e6-5d10-a9a3-70e0207f524d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eced41ae-3fab-5c67-a3bf-aedcd9f64978" :question/id #uuid "eced41ae-3fab-5c67-a3bf-aedcd9f64978" :question/math-academy-id "q-105382" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two particles of mass ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ that are at a distance $r$ from each other is given by the formula ${F}_{\\text{G}}=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}},$ where $G$ is the gravitational constant measured in $\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}},$ masses ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are measured in kilograms, and the distance $r$ is measured in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for the gravitational force." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of gravitational force by substituting the given unit of the gravitational constant, mass, and distance into the formula and simplifying it.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{\\text{G}} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{kg}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\cdot \\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\,\\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the gravitational force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1658fceb-3d29-5e01-ae74-ca2195773a25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1658fceb-3d29-5e01-ae74-ca2195773a25" :answer-field/id #uuid "1658fceb-3d29-5e01-ae74-ca2195773a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" "answer-e5473d53-bc7d-58b4-97c6-6181108fc8a7" "answer-dc62581e-c5c7-5bf5-993f-988441a4c219" "answer-232f8a61-dc2b-5713-b945-81e4ad13440b" "answer-4d9cd203-1c3a-51d2-9094-86b40b32f3d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" :answer/id #uuid "23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5473d53-bc7d-58b4-97c6-6181108fc8a7" :answer/id #uuid "e5473d53-bc7d-58b4-97c6-6181108fc8a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc62581e-c5c7-5bf5-993f-988441a4c219" :answer/id #uuid "dc62581e-c5c7-5bf5-993f-988441a4c219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}\\,\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-232f8a61-dc2b-5713-b945-81e4ad13440b" :answer/id #uuid "232f8a61-dc2b-5713-b945-81e4ad13440b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d9cd203-1c3a-51d2-9094-86b40b32f3d3" :answer/id #uuid "4d9cd203-1c3a-51d2-9094-86b40b32f3d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-177c4027-65c0-5159-869d-c7c5edbf2c0b" :question/id #uuid "177c4027-65c0-5159-869d-c7c5edbf2c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105441" :question/problem "A car dealership with $7$ sellers sells $50$ cars in a month. The owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{c}{s}\n$$\n\nto determine, on average, the number of cars sold by each seller, where $c$ is the total of cars sold, and $s$ is the total number of sellers.\n\nFind an appropriate measurement unit for $N.$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $50$ and $7.$ They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $c$ represents $\\text{cars}$ and $s$ represents $\\text{sellers},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{c}{s}$ is $\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6021314-f875-5954-b86b-11e994619d15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6021314-f875-5954-b86b-11e994619d15" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6021314-f875-5954-b86b-11e994619d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" :answer-field/choices ["answer-adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" "answer-eca4b609-64f1-5eff-a923-cb5c2dedb208" "answer-b3a71fc2-2e3f-5004-8f69-4b70fb5b946a" "answer-75d4f1b5-f48c-5114-bb9b-77b789e0e67d" "answer-465d914d-5e5f-5208-9d20-21251f1b74ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" :answer/id #uuid "adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eca4b609-64f1-5eff-a923-cb5c2dedb208" :answer/id #uuid "eca4b609-64f1-5eff-a923-cb5c2dedb208" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cars}}{{\\text{seller}}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a71fc2-2e3f-5004-8f69-4b70fb5b946a" :answer/id #uuid "b3a71fc2-2e3f-5004-8f69-4b70fb5b946a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{sellers}}{\\text{car}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75d4f1b5-f48c-5114-bb9b-77b789e0e67d" :answer/id #uuid "75d4f1b5-f48c-5114-bb9b-77b789e0e67d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{cars}\\cdot \\text{seller}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-465d914d-5e5f-5208-9d20-21251f1b74ae" :answer/id #uuid "465d914d-5e5f-5208-9d20-21251f1b74ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{cars}\\cdot {\\text{seller}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff3d9c94-77b4-51bb-806f-e93681c391b2" :question/id #uuid "ff3d9c94-77b4-51bb-806f-e93681c391b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-46090" :question/problem "A printer prints $1\\,250$ letters in $60$ minutes. To determine the number of letters that the printer prints on average, the owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{l}{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the number of letters printed and $t$ the time in minutes.\n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $N?$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $1\\,250$ and $60.$ They do not matter, if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $l$ represents $\\text{letters}$ and $t$ represents $\\text{minute},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{l}{t}$ is $\\frac{\\text{letters}}{\\text{minute}}.$ In words, this is 'letters per minute'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44a81c31-3d9d-5c47-8101-840c572bf6c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44a81c31-3d9d-5c47-8101-840c572bf6c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "44a81c31-3d9d-5c47-8101-840c572bf6c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c009fba3-f728-5ed7-aafe-3f93f120520d" "answer-2a6e619d-74a8-5230-bbd2-35edd9e4cf43" "answer-21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" "answer-cd23376a-66eb-56d2-acfe-654ce73059e8" "answer-937a97e5-808c-5a30-8887-12355f82c9c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c009fba3-f728-5ed7-aafe-3f93f120520d" :answer/id #uuid "c009fba3-f728-5ed7-aafe-3f93f120520d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic letters per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a6e619d-74a8-5230-bbd2-35edd9e4cf43" :answer/id #uuid "2a6e619d-74a8-5230-bbd2-35edd9e4cf43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Letters per cubic minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" :answer/id #uuid "21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Letters per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd23376a-66eb-56d2-acfe-654ce73059e8" :answer/id #uuid "cd23376a-66eb-56d2-acfe-654ce73059e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Letters per squared minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-937a97e5-808c-5a30-8887-12355f82c9c7" :answer/id #uuid "937a97e5-808c-5a30-8887-12355f82c9c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Minutes per letter" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139596 :tutorial/content "The formula for the speed $S$ of a moving object is\n\n$$\nS=\\dfrac{D}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the distance covered, and $T$ is the time taken.\n\nFor example, if a car travels a distance of $600\\,\\textrm{m}$ within a time of $30\\,\\textrm{s},$ at constant speed, then we can find the speed of the car using the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{D}{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{30} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what is the unit of speed? We can find it by substituting the units for distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{D}{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the car is $20\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$ When the unit has a quotient like this, we say \"meters *per* second.\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139597 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can be given a formula with values whose units are not the standard ones we know.\n\nFor instance, suppose the formula for a person's popularity $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{2f}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is the number of friends that person has, and $s$ is the number of siblings they have.\n\nIf we want to find the unit of popularity, then we need to substitute the units for the number of friends ($f$) and the number of siblings ($s$) into the formula.\n\n- The units for the number of friends is '$\\textrm{friends}$' since we say 'I have $5$ friends'.\n\n- Similarly, the units for the number of siblings is '$\\textrm{siblings}.$'\n\nSubstituting these units into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{2f}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\text{friends}}{\\text{siblings}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{friends}}{\\text{siblings}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{friends}}{\\textrm{siblings}}$, or \"friends per sibling.\" Here we ignore the constant $2$ since it does not affect the units." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138590 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-58a9cc29-6dde-501b-9df3-e43459e6c342" "question-67c34d9e-6885-5f7c-abc2-3a7d77eaaea5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-58a9cc29-6dde-501b-9df3-e43459e6c342" :question/id #uuid "58a9cc29-6dde-501b-9df3-e43459e6c342" :question/math-academy-id "q-95331" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8x{e}^{-2x}-8{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}=8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x)\\,⟹\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,\\infty ),$ so we keep them.\n\n- We've already considered the endpoint $x=0.$\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4\\cdot {0}^{2}{e}^{-0}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =4\\cdot {1}^{2}{e}^{-2}=4{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(1)=4{e}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d9ba206c-1c09-5d73-aac2-7ac035ea537b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d9ba206c-1c09-5d73-aac2-7ac035ea537b" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ba206c-1c09-5d73-aac2-7ac035ea537b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b35f84fc-e294-5051-9663-cb93c85382e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a885735-a293-5a6e-b9f0-e71c16d2d6ba" "answer-b35f84fc-e294-5051-9663-cb93c85382e3" "answer-31890b7f-ed6a-5164-9d49-0ee7fd1e023b" "answer-22e3400e-46e9-5910-8fa1-93c60030b219" "answer-1cd919b6-d9a5-5867-a6da-8315148dae77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a885735-a293-5a6e-b9f0-e71c16d2d6ba" :answer/id #uuid "7a885735-a293-5a6e-b9f0-e71c16d2d6ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b35f84fc-e294-5051-9663-cb93c85382e3" :answer/id #uuid "b35f84fc-e294-5051-9663-cb93c85382e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31890b7f-ed6a-5164-9d49-0ee7fd1e023b" :answer/id #uuid "31890b7f-ed6a-5164-9d49-0ee7fd1e023b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22e3400e-46e9-5910-8fa1-93c60030b219" :answer/id #uuid "22e3400e-46e9-5910-8fa1-93c60030b219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cd919b6-d9a5-5867-a6da-8315148dae77" :answer/id #uuid "1cd919b6-d9a5-5867-a6da-8315148dae77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a2c219c-ccba-5ebb-bb19-2884f7df2aa0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a2c219c-ccba-5ebb-bb19-2884f7df2aa0" :question/id #uuid "3a2c219c-ccba-5ebb-bb19-2884f7df2aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95376" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3\\pi $ is $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only three lie within the interval $[0,3\\pi ].$ These are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3\\pi .$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(3\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b20b945-8756-5419-8b95-3531f4fb01ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b20b945-8756-5419-8b95-3531f4fb01ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b20b945-8756-5419-8b95-3531f4fb01ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6caa4140-d1ac-55c9-a193-10e41b581be0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-562298c6-72ee-5938-8d58-eb91a47a9605" "answer-f65de3f9-aea8-580f-b207-a07101ca1006" "answer-8af47cc7-2ec7-566f-b27b-062280eae539" "answer-dcbb8c12-c911-598d-ab85-055bef131cb5" "answer-6caa4140-d1ac-55c9-a193-10e41b581be0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-562298c6-72ee-5938-8d58-eb91a47a9605" :answer/id #uuid "562298c6-72ee-5938-8d58-eb91a47a9605" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f65de3f9-aea8-580f-b207-a07101ca1006" :answer/id #uuid "f65de3f9-aea8-580f-b207-a07101ca1006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2},3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8af47cc7-2ec7-566f-b27b-062280eae539" :answer/id #uuid "8af47cc7-2ec7-566f-b27b-062280eae539" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcbb8c12-c911-598d-ab85-055bef131cb5" :answer/id #uuid "dcbb8c12-c911-598d-ab85-055bef131cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6caa4140-d1ac-55c9-a193-10e41b581be0" :answer/id #uuid "6caa4140-d1ac-55c9-a193-10e41b581be0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb98fa7b-b950-5181-aa33-ef959985aa54" "question-3ee32b00-5c8a-53d2-a621-006bbc7ec2f3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb98fa7b-b950-5181-aa33-ef959985aa54" :question/id #uuid "cb98fa7b-b950-5181-aa33-ef959985aa54" :question/math-academy-id "q-126301" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5180db140f9567f6863eac3a4bedc498830c2f3e427d04480f61dfa3a64b3ce.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=4,7,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $4,7$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/13/13de2513361cca92312190fb75d3c42a508de46efdc753ee6f6257c72e23c3e6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8bbfbca-ebe9-54a2-a89e-68cf7149b2de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8bbfbca-ebe9-54a2-a89e-68cf7149b2de" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8bbfbca-ebe9-54a2-a89e-68cf7149b2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a3f7066d-8c76-5171-acaa-21fb48763b2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b94bc8ac-78b2-5723-8652-c01c4e2aec68" "answer-49d087e9-36d9-5c1d-977a-51607b4bc92e" "answer-f27b6be5-6904-575d-8c49-1d0b888c6e4f" "answer-a3f7066d-8c76-5171-acaa-21fb48763b2d" "answer-a42d220b-ba0b-5a9c-97ee-61a553ddce1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b94bc8ac-78b2-5723-8652-c01c4e2aec68" :answer/id #uuid "b94bc8ac-78b2-5723-8652-c01c4e2aec68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49d087e9-36d9-5c1d-977a-51607b4bc92e" :answer/id #uuid "49d087e9-36d9-5c1d-977a-51607b4bc92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f27b6be5-6904-575d-8c49-1d0b888c6e4f" :answer/id #uuid "f27b6be5-6904-575d-8c49-1d0b888c6e4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3f7066d-8c76-5171-acaa-21fb48763b2d" :answer/id #uuid "a3f7066d-8c76-5171-acaa-21fb48763b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4,7,10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a42d220b-ba0b-5a9c-97ee-61a553ddce1b" :answer/id #uuid "a42d220b-ba0b-5a9c-97ee-61a553ddce1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7,10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ee32b00-5c8a-53d2-a621-006bbc7ec2f3" :question/id #uuid "3ee32b00-5c8a-53d2-a621-006bbc7ec2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126318" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcb83928fdc185929add5a26c49289834c70af2b85aa3a856dcecdba73138788.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=-1,3,9.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1,3$ and $9.$\n\n![](images/6e/6e5f9b96a81ee467251cbe7f1792b3bb8c58bb454e2ec3684173ca798ca0f9d9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-120d7af0-9d99-54b0-9a46-fee359cefc49"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-120d7af0-9d99-54b0-9a46-fee359cefc49" :answer-field/id #uuid "120d7af0-9d99-54b0-9a46-fee359cefc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d74f2c57-186b-515a-aace-7e2faca7126f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fed614a-a35c-5c78-8079-e19a4b70e2a8" "answer-d74f2c57-186b-515a-aace-7e2faca7126f" "answer-40166c8f-84c5-52fa-ada2-77e8a55af142" "answer-3a042cdc-f2a9-5a30-bd6d-8da210b1ffee" "answer-e6f6e3d5-7b3a-510a-a0b4-233f40962a24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fed614a-a35c-5c78-8079-e19a4b70e2a8" :answer/id #uuid "2fed614a-a35c-5c78-8079-e19a4b70e2a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d74f2c57-186b-515a-aace-7e2faca7126f" :answer/id #uuid "d74f2c57-186b-515a-aace-7e2faca7126f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,3,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40166c8f-84c5-52fa-ada2-77e8a55af142" :answer/id #uuid "40166c8f-84c5-52fa-ada2-77e8a55af142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a042cdc-f2a9-5a30-bd6d-8da210b1ffee" :answer/id #uuid "3a042cdc-f2a9-5a30-bd6d-8da210b1ffee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1,3,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6f6e3d5-7b3a-510a-a0b4-233f40962a24" :answer/id #uuid "e6f6e3d5-7b3a-510a-a0b4-233f40962a24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-67c34d9e-6885-5f7c-abc2-3a7d77eaaea5" :question/id #uuid "67c34d9e-6885-5f7c-abc2-3a7d77eaaea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126319" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 7 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $1\\le x\\le 7$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{24}x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=1,7.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=1,7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ f(7) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 7=\\frac{7}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(7)=\\frac{7}{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e45ad8ad-0b7a-5fe0-93c7-2567d8e280e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e45ad8ad-0b7a-5fe0-93c7-2567d8e280e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "e45ad8ad-0b7a-5fe0-93c7-2567d8e280e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b1502135-3cf5-544d-b180-cf421c12bfbe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fdf54c2-2921-5ec5-b47f-e60a64e832fc" "answer-d667db61-2218-508d-88b5-9f484b8424be" "answer-43a25aad-1096-53f8-a1b0-ffe4ce93e7f0" "answer-b1502135-3cf5-544d-b180-cf