e]} {:db/id 17592186389734 :question/problem "$$\n{{\\{1,2\\}\\cap \\{3\\}=\\{1,2,3\\}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{9\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∧B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is false, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\{1,2\\}\\cap \\{3\\}=\\{1,2,3\\}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{9\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{false}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7eb8fb9-95e2-5b65-af61-0f6fe58c75b8" :question/id #uuid "e7eb8fb9-95e2-5b65-af61-0f6fe58c75b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-178162" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\overset{P}{\\overset{⏞}{0\\le -1}}\\,\\overset{∨}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{or}}}\\,\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{3\\,|\\,7}}}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,Q}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{14\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∨Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∨Q)∨R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∨Q$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∨R & ≡\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62493ac5-f15b-51cb-8a00-6c91b7e0c5cd" "answer-field-bf4f5979-2231-543e-a839-0854443bf580" "answer-field-c9feaa5e-9db9-5e7e-a63b-25953d48d1fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62493ac5-f15b-51cb-8a00-6c91b7e0c5cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "62493ac5-f15b-51cb-8a00-6c91b7e0c5cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2046c2fd-a8d4-54d2-8ffd-95ecd612b6eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2046c2fd-a8d4-54d2-8ffd-95ecd612b6eb" "answer-46b2322d-6809-5d8b-883d-878f30de914e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2046c2fd-a8d4-54d2-8ffd-95ecd612b6eb" :answer/id #uuid "2046c2fd-a8d4-54d2-8ffd-95ecd612b6eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46b2322d-6809-5d8b-883d-878f30de914e" :answer/id #uuid "46b2322d-6809-5d8b-883d-878f30de914e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf4f5979-2231-543e-a839-0854443bf580" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf4f5979-2231-543e-a839-0854443bf580" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1d0928b9-6aee-5a24-8532-dfa31a856083" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d0928b9-6aee-5a24-8532-dfa31a856083" "answer-f9d30708-c054-535c-8d52-04c500ecca7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d0928b9-6aee-5a24-8532-dfa31a856083" :answer/id #uuid "1d0928b9-6aee-5a24-8532-dfa31a856083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9d30708-c054-535c-8d52-04c500ecca7f" :answer/id #uuid "f9d30708-c054-535c-8d52-04c500ecca7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9feaa5e-9db9-5e7e-a63b-25953d48d1fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9feaa5e-9db9-5e7e-a63b-25953d48d1fe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-fa0877b1-0fd1-5c32-9fec-6b40f84addfc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa0877b1-0fd1-5c32-9fec-6b40f84addfc" "answer-a5fd4d05-59ac-59bc-a081-aec213d99bba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa0877b1-0fd1-5c32-9fec-6b40f84addfc" :answer/id #uuid "fa0877b1-0fd1-5c32-9fec-6b40f84addfc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5fd4d05-59ac-59bc-a081-aec213d99bba" :answer/id #uuid "a5fd4d05-59ac-59bc-a081-aec213d99bba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbfbaab7-10b5-57a8-a26d-3c15f79b1087" :question/id #uuid "bbfbaab7-10b5-57a8-a26d-3c15f79b1087" :question/math-academy-id "q-175930" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\overset{P}{\\overset{⏞}{\\{1,2\\}\\cup \\{2\\}=\\{1,2\\}}}\\,\\overset{∨}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{or}}}\\,\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{11\\text{ is prime}}}}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,Q}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is a perfect square}}_{⏟}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∨Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∨Q)∧R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∨Q$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∧R & ≡\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{true})}}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55ff92d9-197d-537e-a612-7fa2b44af8b2" "answer-field-1c280642-33c4-5bed-b4d3-b3f46ad0d1a8" "answer-field-d153c1d9-5604-5d8d-a941-1ffa64d648ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55ff92d9-197d-537e-a612-7fa2b44af8b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "55ff92d9-197d-537e-a612-7fa2b44af8b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-91f2d60f-5b20-5249-b392-f82fcb256770" :answer-field/choices ["answer-493e6d2e-e984-5db7-8e87-4c3e03dcd6c2" "answer-91f2d60f-5b20-5249-b392-f82fcb256770"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493e6d2e-e984-5db7-8e87-4c3e03dcd6c2" :answer/id #uuid "493e6d2e-e984-5db7-8e87-4c3e03dcd6c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91f2d60f-5b20-5249-b392-f82fcb256770" :answer/id #uuid "91f2d60f-5b20-5249-b392-f82fcb256770" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c280642-33c4-5bed-b4d3-b3f46ad0d1a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c280642-33c4-5bed-b4d3-b3f46ad0d1a8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-12345e4a-d887-537f-b77e-dad11466435e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12345e4a-d887-537f-b77e-dad11466435e" "answer-4a2de7e6-e549-5c70-96e5-8a3192abc611"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12345e4a-d887-537f-b77e-dad11466435e" :answer/id #uuid "12345e4a-d887-537f-b77e-dad11466435e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2de7e6-e549-5c70-96e5-8a3192abc611" :answer/id #uuid "4a2de7e6-e549-5c70-96e5-8a3192abc611" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d153c1d9-5604-5d8d-a941-1ffa64d648ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "d153c1d9-5604-5d8d-a941-1ffa64d648ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-84f1d02d-fab2-534e-ac78-73d235fea8dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84f1d02d-fab2-534e-ac78-73d235fea8dc" "answer-bcaa3233-b273-5d33-96e5-df8a354675a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84f1d02d-fab2-534e-ac78-73d235fea8dc" :answer/id #uuid "84f1d02d-fab2-534e-ac78-73d235fea8dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcaa3233-b273-5d33-96e5-df8a354675a8" :answer/id #uuid "bcaa3233-b273-5d33-96e5-df8a354675a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f1de2e0-3e98-599c-9d88-57f6ce468e91" :question/id #uuid "5f1de2e0-3e98-599c-9d88-57f6ce468e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-178160" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\{1,2\\}\\cup \\{3\\}=\\{1,2,3\\}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{0\\in Z}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\text{ is positive}}}}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∧\\,R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $Q∧R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $P∧(Q∧R)$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is true. Therefore, $Q∧R$ is true, and the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡\\text{true}∧\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{true})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72b36df2-d89f-5abe-858c-1857f190329a" "answer-field-c2df1e71-a31a-5d0a-9914-84a4c2f5d9f9" "answer-field-ccd7f2b5-f27d-5687-a14e-af851e4f6035"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72b36df2-d89f-5abe-858c-1857f190329a" :answer-field/id #uuid "72b36df2-d89f-5abe-858c-1857f190329a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6ff306a6-5eea-5635-9260-d6587a94cf99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eed73199-0233-5fe9-9579-5c3beb34f615" "answer-6ff306a6-5eea-5635-9260-d6587a94cf99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eed73199-0233-5fe9-9579-5c3beb34f615" :answer/id #uuid "eed73199-0233-5fe9-9579-5c3beb34f615" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ff306a6-5eea-5635-9260-d6587a94cf99" :answer/id #uuid "6ff306a6-5eea-5635-9260-d6587a94cf99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2df1e71-a31a-5d0a-9914-84a4c2f5d9f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2df1e71-a31a-5d0a-9914-84a4c2f5d9f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-56286c0e-5369-57ac-a6ca-3c929622e2c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2811c57-55de-52d3-9b49-33ee017e90c1" "answer-56286c0e-5369-57ac-a6ca-3c929622e2c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2811c57-55de-52d3-9b49-33ee017e90c1" :answer/id #uuid "a2811c57-55de-52d3-9b49-33ee017e90c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56286c0e-5369-57ac-a6ca-3c929622e2c8" :answer/id #uuid "56286c0e-5369-57ac-a6ca-3c929622e2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccd7f2b5-f27d-5687-a14e-af851e4f6035" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd7f2b5-f27d-5687-a14e-af851e4f6035" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1a3356ed-3529-5722-8c94-522595f71603" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b094bbd7-3104-509c-95df-83d8c6d7df4e" "answer-1a3356ed-3529-5722-8c94-522595f71603"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b094bbd7-3104-509c-95df-83d8c6d7df4e" :answer/id #uuid "b094bbd7-3104-509c-95df-83d8c6d7df4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a3356ed-3529-5722-8c94-522595f71603" :answer/id #uuid "1a3356ed-3529-5722-8c94-522595f71603" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055061 :question/problem "$$\n{{\\overset{P}{\\overset{⏞}{{3}^{2}=9}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{2\\text{ is rational}}}}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∧\\,Q}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∧Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∧Q)∧R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is true. Therefore, $P∧Q$ is true, and the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∧R & ≡\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{true})}}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051859 :answer-field/correct 17592186051860 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051862 :answer-field/correct 17592186051863 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051865 :answer-field/correct 17592186051867 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056005 :tutorial/content "A **compound statement** is a sentence that consists of two or more statements separated by logical connectors, such as the word \"and\" or the word \"or.\"\n\nFor example, the following sentence is a compound statement:\n\n$P: 2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive \n\nThe first statement is \"$2$ is an integer,\" and the second is \"$2$ is positive.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"and.\"\n\nThe following sentence is also a compound statement:\n\n$7$ is odd **or** $7$ is even \n\nIn this case, the first statement is \"$7$ is odd,\" and the second statement is \"$7$ is even.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"or.\" \n\nWe can express the logical connectives \"and\" and \"or\" using symbols:\n\n- The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\". Notice that it resembles \"A\" for \"And.\"\n\n- The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nLet's translate the following sentence into symbolic form:\n\n$2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive \n\nTo start, we introduce the letters $A$ and $B$ to denote each individual statement:\n\n- $A:$ $2$ is an integer\n\n- $B:$  $2$ is positive\n\nThen, our sentence becomes\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is an integer} }_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is positive} }_B,\n$$\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nFinally, note the following terminology:\n\n- The statement \"$P$ and $Q$\" is called the **conjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$\n\n- The statement \"$P$ or $Q$\" is called the **disjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056029 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$\nA\\land B.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\" \n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\land C\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056072 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054914 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186135922 :answer-field/choices 17592186135923] [:db/retract 17592186135922 :answer-field/choices 17592186135924] [:db/retract 17592186135922 :answer-field/choices 17592186135925] [:db/retract 17592186135922 :answer-field/choices 17592186135926] [:db/retract 17592186135922 :answer-field/choices 17592186135927] {:db/id 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da73dbf4-60d5-5996-a49e-cd36cf80dd15" "question-1734fbf3-8c46-59f6-b44b-50223df284ea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138282 :question/problem "Let $C,$ $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E.$ If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/30/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =CD+DE \\\\ 8 & =6+DE \\\\ 8-6 & =DE \\\\ 2 & =DE\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $DE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-64c26ddd-268e-5f64-a3fd-ce3f88c6ef7f" "question-8d7c6a20-2db9-5df2-9d3a-9d9b2f89a1a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138283 :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the measure of $\\overline{BC}?$\n\n![](images/54/543c7c26c7d56d8ff10f4ece71b74bb5f04edf59fddb6f2c0df549848000abaf.png)" :question/worked-solution "The segment addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 21 & =5+BC+12 \\\\ 21 & =17+BC \\\\ 21-17 & =BC \\\\ BC & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d31182c-3033-5d05-9db8-c0bd99386616" "question-ce45e183-4121-5c7a-893b-6d26e45f745c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138284 :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the value of $x?$\n\n![](images/3f/3fe1e3d1e6f89af1050d0437379a811eabb19212be36f0fecd91ddfaca2594ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=30, \\quad AB=x+5, \\quad BC=13, \\quad CD=5x,\n$$\n\n \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 30 & =(x+5)+13+5x \\\\ 30 & =6x+18 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ec2f525c-530a-5760-a56d-781b9e8e9599"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138285 :question/problem "In the diagram below, the length of the line segment $\\overline{AD}$ is $8d+12,$ $AB = 4d+4,$ and $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}.$ Express the length of $\\overline{AC}$ in terms of $d.$\n\n![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(8d+12)-(4d+4) \\\\  & =4d+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4d+8) \\\\  & =2d+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\  & =(4d+4)+(2d+4) \\\\  & =6d+8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-da73dbf4-60d5-5996-a49e-cd36cf80dd15" :question/id #uuid "da73dbf4-60d5-5996-a49e-cd36cf80dd15" :question/math-academy-id "q-72037" :question/problem "Let $P$, $Q$ and $R$ be three collinear points such that $PQ=7\\text{ cm}$ and $QR=13\\text{ cm}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P$ **cannot** lie between $Q$ and $R$\n\nII. $Q$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $R$\n\nIII. $R$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\nIf $P$ is between $Q$ and $R$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =PQ+PR \\\\ 13 & =7+PR \\\\ 13-7 & =PR \\\\ PR & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/d7/d7396a24e0e8884195184a4fca2b4e0fd9cfc2462249c7dc3a0c66901d062c5f.png)\n\nTherefore, $P$ can lie between $Q$ and $R,$ so statement I is false.\n\nIf $Q$ is between $P$ and $R$, then again, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =PQ+QR \\\\  & =7+13 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dc899f0b5d08bf4a5b5e1833e62e4a3290c8da83dbd376ed51d185c1ed08e94.png)\n\nTherefore, $Q$ can lie between $P$ and $R,$ so statement II is false.\n\nIf $R$ is between $P$ and $Q$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we would have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =PR+QR \\\\ 7 & =PR+13 \\\\ PR & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can't happen since the length of a segment can't be negative. \n\nTherefore, $R$ cannot lie between $P$ and $Q,$ so statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43d96bdb-5516-5f1b-b865-c20f14f73879"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43d96bdb-5516-5f1b-b865-c20f14f73879" :answer-field/id #uuid "43d96bdb-5516-5f1b-b865-c20f14f73879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6e3b7f4-fcf4-590a-87b6-7a75fdf58038" "answer-2d752876-29d0-538c-b43b-c0eca6b040a8" "answer-ae3a56e8-fb91-53c7-b022-e3a8f6b2db0b" "answer-6fcb0ff8-cf1e-5af3-911b-4cded950d7c6" "answer-256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6e3b7f4-fcf4-590a-87b6-7a75fdf58038" :answer/id #uuid "f6e3b7f4-fcf4-590a-87b6-7a75fdf58038" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d752876-29d0-538c-b43b-c0eca6b040a8" :answer/id #uuid "2d752876-29d0-538c-b43b-c0eca6b040a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3a56e8-fb91-53c7-b022-e3a8f6b2db0b" :answer/id #uuid "ae3a56e8-fb91-53c7-b022-e3a8f6b2db0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fcb0ff8-cf1e-5af3-911b-4cded950d7c6" :answer/id #uuid "6fcb0ff8-cf1e-5af3-911b-4cded950d7c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13" :answer/id #uuid "256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1734fbf3-8c46-59f6-b44b-50223df284ea" :question/id #uuid "1734fbf3-8c46-59f6-b44b-50223df284ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-25786" :question/problem "Let $P$, $Q$ and $R$ be three collinear points such that $PQ=11\\text{ cm}$ and $QR=16\\text{ cm}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P$ **cannot** lie between $Q$ and $R$\n\nII. $Q$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $R$\n\nIII. $R$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\nIf $P$ is between $Q$ and $R$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =PQ+PR \\\\ 16 & =11+PR \\\\ 16-11 & =PR \\\\ PR & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/10a73ef77e6f4367b971465ab6be0a4decdb8a36a2931944fbe10be11c92475f.png)\n\nTherefore, $P$ can lie between $Q$ and $R,$ so statement I is false.\n\nIf $Q$ is between $P$ and $R$, then again, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =PQ+QR \\\\  & =11+16 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/7b/7b8d7c69c2a8d20e3c9a7e06a5cc5d3d456b117f4e7d7ce708cacf27fece0d68.png)\n\nTherefore, $Q$ can lie between $P$ and $R,$ so statement II is false.\n\nIf $R$ is between $P$ and $Q$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we would have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =PR+QR \\\\ 11 & =PR+16 \\\\ PR & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can't happen since the length of a segment can't be negative. \n\nTherefore, $R$ cannot lie between $P$ and $Q,$ so statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e1cdf107-7e54-5936-9f13-a265112a8b8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1cdf107-7e54-5936-9f13-a265112a8b8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cdf107-7e54-5936-9f13-a265112a8b8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1cb27e54-c3a6-54a4-81e6-dff4e2cfb317" "answer-466d6286-724d-5d86-ba8f-fa6e2c735aaf" "answer-db3f6572-bf17-549c-9b8b-8c0a3cb923e8" "answer-978bb03f-1aca-511f-aa41-17c7b150bd8c" "answer-0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cb27e54-c3a6-54a4-81e6-dff4e2cfb317" :answer/id #uuid "1cb27e54-c3a6-54a4-81e6-dff4e2cfb317" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-466d6286-724d-5d86-ba8f-fa6e2c735aaf" :answer/id #uuid "466d6286-724d-5d86-ba8f-fa6e2c735aaf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db3f6572-bf17-549c-9b8b-8c0a3cb923e8" :answer/id #uuid "db3f6572-bf17-549c-9b8b-8c0a3cb923e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-978bb03f-1aca-511f-aa41-17c7b150bd8c" :answer/id #uuid "978bb03f-1aca-511f-aa41-17c7b150bd8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f" :answer/id #uuid "0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64c26ddd-268e-5f64-a3fd-ce3f88c6ef7f" :question/id #uuid "64c26ddd-268e-5f64-a3fd-ce3f88c6ef7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72040" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0a3fc0be34d5a9d94d1f0d4e8a1b30449a5a23bd0401b079156404fe91c043f.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" :question/worked-solution "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 46 & =AB+25+11 \\\\ 46 & =AB+36 \\\\ 46-36 & =AB \\\\ AB & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55521a7c-750c-515c-af51-bff169578548"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55521a7c-750c-515c-af51-bff169578548" :answer-field/id #uuid "55521a7c-750c-515c-af51-bff169578548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" "answer-3a5ce289-87a3-5a09-a506-c6f500d39cbe" "answer-57d741f4-5570-5294-a7f3-0dc358306cc5" "answer-7acc59f4-8494-583b-8bc4-7a1c4912842f" "answer-62887a28-853e-5233-bc37-5b59c6cf567d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" :answer/id #uuid "a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5ce289-87a3-5a09-a506-c6f500d39cbe" :answer/id #uuid "3a5ce289-87a3-5a09-a506-c6f500d39cbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57d741f4-5570-5294-a7f3-0dc358306cc5" :answer/id #uuid "57d741f4-5570-5294-a7f3-0dc358306cc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7acc59f4-8494-583b-8bc4-7a1c4912842f" :answer/id #uuid "7acc59f4-8494-583b-8bc4-7a1c4912842f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62887a28-853e-5233-bc37-5b59c6cf567d" :answer/id #uuid "62887a28-853e-5233-bc37-5b59c6cf567d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d7c6a20-2db9-5df2-9d3a-9d9b2f89a1a3" :question/id #uuid "8d7c6a20-2db9-5df2-9d3a-9d9b2f89a1a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114224" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e151e2518ad2297f86aa25ebdd395ac62c2d05f9e6781d0d82e66a10418aa5a.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{CD}?$" :question/worked-solution "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that \n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 54 & =17+22+CD \\\\ 54 & =39+CD \\\\ 54-39 & =CD \\\\ CD & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-732b3c8c-f12e-5758-98b7-59669894ad91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-732b3c8c-f12e-5758-98b7-59669894ad91" :answer-field/id #uuid "732b3c8c-f12e-5758-98b7-59669894ad91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3388ca0c-124f-5d4f-985f-3ed9da4c4b54" "answer-1dcc7d71-0d06-530d-8640-754ddf2dbe36" "answer-0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" "answer-65eec781-812e-52cb-8c3d-3be3f6a25654" "answer-8dfff003-e842-5741-8e64-38def0061011"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3388ca0c-124f-5d4f-985f-3ed9da4c4b54" :answer/id #uuid "3388ca0c-124f-5d4f-985f-3ed9da4c4b54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dcc7d71-0d06-530d-8640-754ddf2dbe36" :answer/id #uuid "1dcc7d71-0d06-530d-8640-754ddf2dbe36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" :answer/id #uuid "0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65eec781-812e-52cb-8c3d-3be3f6a25654" :answer/id #uuid "65eec781-812e-52cb-8c3d-3be3f6a25654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dfff003-e842-5741-8e64-38def0061011" :answer/id #uuid "8dfff003-e842-5741-8e64-38def0061011" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d31182c-3033-5d05-9db8-c0bd99386616" :question/id #uuid "3d31182c-3033-5d05-9db8-c0bd99386616" :question/math-academy-id "q-114236" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2e722f9b566c903edaae9c90e2f0cac4f6b1a55e74b0c31724a2f675e5d16f5.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=69,\\,AB=5x-2,\\,BC=x+2,\\,CD=27,\n$$\n\n  \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 69 & =(5x-2)+(x+2)+27 \\\\ 69 & =6x+27 \\\\ 6x & =42 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5173e51-80b0-5079-933a-f2cad1712477"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5173e51-80b0-5079-933a-f2cad1712477" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5173e51-80b0-5079-933a-f2cad1712477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1abf0bf-f1db-5257-9b0e-796cd5006fd4" "answer-380ed133-1860-5fee-99ea-3438d7d1c75b" "answer-1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" "answer-55ebe43f-ac41-5bfb-b1c4-f25e69da581b" "answer-2077f56d-45f8-5b3a-b158-dc49085ead2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1abf0bf-f1db-5257-9b0e-796cd5006fd4" :answer/id #uuid "d1abf0bf-f1db-5257-9b0e-796cd5006fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-380ed133-1860-5fee-99ea-3438d7d1c75b" :answer/id #uuid "380ed133-1860-5fee-99ea-3438d7d1c75b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" :answer/id #uuid "1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55ebe43f-ac41-5bfb-b1c4-f25e69da581b" :answer/id #uuid "55ebe43f-ac41-5bfb-b1c4-f25e69da581b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2077f56d-45f8-5b3a-b158-dc49085ead2a" :answer/id #uuid "2077f56d-45f8-5b3a-b158-dc49085ead2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ce45e183-4121-5c7a-893b-6d26e45f745c" :question/id #uuid "ce45e183-4121-5c7a-893b-6d26e45f745c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67596" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a400fb78a04d7a097f59b1ce38919362b64a37660b34313584c58c03547c6785.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=33,\\,AB=4x,\\,BC=16-x,\\,CD=8,\n$$\n\n   \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 33 & =4x+(16-x)+8 \\\\ 33 & =3x+24 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75eee19d-3154-5c95-b3ac-781e034cbd00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75eee19d-3154-5c95-b3ac-781e034cbd00" :answer-field/id #uuid "75eee19d-3154-5c95-b3ac-781e034cbd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" "answer-c33467e9-57e7-5c90-afed-9d4db460f4a9" "answer-d406d6d6-8c33-5f59-b805-134acba76c2f" "answer-3fb0c933-996e-5d1c-9ef3-4fef3078f7fa" "answer-fb9390d3-6487-55df-8143-0b962be456f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" :answer/id #uuid "4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c33467e9-57e7-5c90-afed-9d4db460f4a9" :answer/id #uuid "c33467e9-57e7-5c90-afed-9d4db460f4a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d406d6d6-8c33-5f59-b805-134acba76c2f" :answer/id #uuid "d406d6d6-8c33-5f59-b805-134acba76c2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fb0c933-996e-5d1c-9ef3-4fef3078f7fa" :answer/id #uuid "3fb0c933-996e-5d1c-9ef3-4fef3078f7fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb9390d3-6487-55df-8143-0b962be456f1" :answer/id #uuid "fb9390d3-6487-55df-8143-0b962be456f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec2f525c-530a-5760-a56d-781b9e8e9599" :question/id #uuid "ec2f525c-530a-5760-a56d-781b9e8e9599" :question/math-academy-id "q-52737" :question/problem "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is $5d+12,$ and $AB=3d+2.$ If $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ find the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(5d+12)-(3d+2) \\\\  & =2d+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =3d+2+\\frac{1}{2}(2d+10) \\\\  & =3d+2+d+5 \\\\  & =4d+7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e07eac4-8d4b-5854-aa03-15bb429fea2d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e07eac4-8d4b-5854-aa03-15bb429fea2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e07eac4-8d4b-5854-aa03-15bb429fea2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd5f1430-ed4b-5ddd-affd-bd4db6a30b78" "answer-34f63758-cb7f-51c4-b50a-99c4f9fc851b" "answer-9cb3529a-7655-57cc-b0dd-1c6b0e08d11f" "answer-3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" "answer-72489cd2-014c-5ed0-9f67-4d47f96fe571"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd5f1430-ed4b-5ddd-affd-bd4db6a30b78" :answer/id #uuid "cd5f1430-ed4b-5ddd-affd-bd4db6a30b78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3d+14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34f63758-cb7f-51c4-b50a-99c4f9fc851b" :answer/id #uuid "34f63758-cb7f-51c4-b50a-99c4f9fc851b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3d+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cb3529a-7655-57cc-b0dd-1c6b0e08d11f" :answer/id #uuid "9cb3529a-7655-57cc-b0dd-1c6b0e08d11f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4d+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" :answer/id #uuid "3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4d+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72489cd2-014c-5ed0-9f67-4d47f96fe571" :answer/id #uuid "72489cd2-014c-5ed0-9f67-4d47f96fe571" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5d+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138829 :question/problem "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is $5d+6,$ and $AD=11d+12.$ If $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ find the length of $\\overset{―}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(11d+12)-(5d+6) \\\\  & =6d+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6d+6) \\\\  & =3d+3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135922 :answer-field/correct "answer-e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc2fb2f6-10e5-5b50-ab79-52fbfef7fbfb" "answer-e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" "answer-2d849ff7-d474-5285-87a4-e389ead36b53" "answer-7f1faa59-ed63-5247-bf8e-b08d97204ba3" "answer-9047f0a9-eeb4-5cec-89a7-53f6ecb81214"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc2fb2f6-10e5-5b50-ab79-52fbfef7fbfb" :answer/id #uuid "fc2fb2f6-10e5-5b50-ab79-52fbfef7fbfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2d+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" :answer/id #uuid "e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3d+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d849ff7-d474-5285-87a4-e389ead36b53" :answer/id #uuid "2d849ff7-d474-5285-87a4-e389ead36b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3d+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f1faa59-ed63-5247-bf8e-b08d97204ba3" :answer/id #uuid "7f1faa59-ed63-5247-bf8e-b08d97204ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6d+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9047f0a9-eeb4-5cec-89a7-53f6ecb81214" :answer/id #uuid "9047f0a9-eeb4-5cec-89a7-53f6ecb81214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6d+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139635 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below. \n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=4\\,cm,\\,BC=2\\,cm,\\,AC=6\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following: \n\n*Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC,$ so the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information $AC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ $AB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ and $BC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 6 & =4+2 \\\\ 6 & =6.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC.$\n\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139636 :tutorial/content "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=3\\,cm,\\,AC=5\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC,$ using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 5 & =3+BC\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3+BC \\\\ BC & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138602 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186162667 :question/answer-fields 17592186161294] {:db/id 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" "question-a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6179c8a4-6c68-54e8-a44c-27a0976388cb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79aa6457-0ad1-5b78-8ab5-2196791a63cf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9582267b-adc4-536b-9335-9619155f3b19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" :question/id #uuid "a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96118" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nII. $U$ is an orthogonal matrix\n\nIII. $Σ$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, $U$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the matrix $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $Σ$ must be diagonal but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d36a171f-653b-5a9e-b0c3-ea8ba71c938e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d36a171f-653b-5a9e-b0c3-ea8ba71c938e" :answer-field/id #uuid "d36a171f-653b-5a9e-b0c3-ea8ba71c938e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0ec482c-c5db-502f-b6f8-1aae7843448d" "answer-285a9780-e86c-548a-adb5-cbb884e8ca6c" "answer-ea634426-335f-5311-b804-0a4c4997b3bc" "answer-2d03c4f6-f195-5e6b-866b-9bb346f9fc68" "answer-319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0ec482c-c5db-502f-b6f8-1aae7843448d" :answer/id #uuid "f0ec482c-c5db-502f-b6f8-1aae7843448d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-285a9780-e86c-548a-adb5-cbb884e8ca6c" :answer/id #uuid "285a9780-e86c-548a-adb5-cbb884e8ca6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea634426-335f-5311-b804-0a4c4997b3bc" :answer/id #uuid "ea634426-335f-5311-b804-0a4c4997b3bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d03c4f6-f195-5e6b-866b-9bb346f9fc68" :answer/id #uuid "2d03c4f6-f195-5e6b-866b-9bb346f9fc68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer/id #uuid "319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7" :question/id #uuid "a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96232" :question/problem "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8e0de73-fab0-5dbe-bb6a-4caec9eea734"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8e0de73-fab0-5dbe-bb6a-4caec9eea734" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e0de73-fab0-5dbe-bb6a-4caec9eea734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9aea7879-dd37-548f-a388-79a0c1cba990" "answer-e0c11591-dfe6-5782-9a57-f4732952a8b8" "answer-4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" "answer-cecc1d54-52d9-5a5f-8a1d-bfaee1aac033" "answer-94c97f0b-950e-53ee-8528-58cf113935e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9aea7879-dd37-548f-a388-79a0c1cba990" :answer/id #uuid "9aea7879-dd37-548f-a388-79a0c1cba990" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c11591-dfe6-5782-9a57-f4732952a8b8" :answer/id #uuid "e0c11591-dfe6-5782-9a57-f4732952a8b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer/id #uuid "4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cecc1d54-52d9-5a5f-8a1d-bfaee1aac033" :answer/id #uuid "cecc1d54-52d9-5a5f-8a1d-bfaee1aac033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94c97f0b-950e-53ee-8528-58cf113935e8" :answer/id #uuid "94c97f0b-950e-53ee-8528-58cf113935e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6179c8a4-6c68-54e8-a44c-27a0976388cb" :question/id #uuid "6179c8a4-6c68-54e8-a44c-27a0976388cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96247" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 34\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d79fe0b-3705-52ed-9f17-ed653d7b53f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d79fe0b-3705-52ed-9f17-ed653d7b53f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d79fe0b-3705-52ed-9f17-ed653d7b53f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c732213-af40-5564-a53d-e06b3be8e467" :answer-field/choices ["answer-debb44c2-f1de-596c-92d5-1f9c41cf8f66" "answer-6473f71a-bb43-5016-b612-4de59067207c" "answer-fb328e06-f7d3-5350-9633-42d96db9c81c" "answer-1c732213-af40-5564-a53d-e06b3be8e467" "answer-c4492d54-81f6-5951-80d9-e8f908a1836d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-debb44c2-f1de-596c-92d5-1f9c41cf8f66" :answer/id #uuid "debb44c2-f1de-596c-92d5-1f9c41cf8f66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6473f71a-bb43-5016-b612-4de59067207c" :answer/id #uuid "6473f71a-bb43-5016-b612-4de59067207c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb328e06-f7d3-5350-9633-42d96db9c81c" :answer/id #uuid "fb328e06-f7d3-5350-9633-42d96db9c81c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c732213-af40-5564-a53d-e06b3be8e467" :answer/id #uuid "1c732213-af40-5564-a53d-e06b3be8e467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4492d54-81f6-5951-80d9-e8f908a1836d" :answer/id #uuid "c4492d54-81f6-5951-80d9-e8f908a1836d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-79aa6457-0ad1-5b78-8ab5-2196791a63cf" :question/id #uuid "79aa6457-0ad1-5b78-8ab5-2196791a63cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95906" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3d56696-4ed6-5583-85eb-387af7ed31be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3d56696-4ed6-5583-85eb-387af7ed31be" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d56696-4ed6-5583-85eb-387af7ed31be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68598054-f8a2-58e6-b3cf-31bcc49b1676" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67a53e3a-1703-5b02-8afb-8f54ac862fec" "answer-68598054-f8a2-58e6-b3cf-31bcc49b1676" "answer-2ae24d11-1cd8-53d0-ad47-5b285f4d0b75" "answer-97e9def0-f134-5fa8-8ec0-c84afe6f5876" "answer-fadf9222-3d73-5771-87bf-4aacd4a26f7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67a53e3a-1703-5b02-8afb-8f54ac862fec" :answer/id #uuid "67a53e3a-1703-5b02-8afb-8f54ac862fec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68598054-f8a2-58e6-b3cf-31bcc49b1676" :answer/id #uuid "68598054-f8a2-58e6-b3cf-31bcc49b1676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ae24d11-1cd8-53d0-ad47-5b285f4d0b75" :answer/id #uuid "2ae24d11-1cd8-53d0-ad47-5b285f4d0b75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97e9def0-f134-5fa8-8ec0-c84afe6f5876" :answer/id #uuid "97e9def0-f134-5fa8-8ec0-c84afe6f5876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fadf9222-3d73-5771-87bf-4aacd4a26f7b" :answer/id #uuid "fadf9222-3d73-5771-87bf-4aacd4a26f7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9582267b-adc4-536b-9335-9619155f3b19" :question/id #uuid "9582267b-adc4-536b-9335-9619155f3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140646" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=64,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-64I & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]-64[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-27 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-64I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20030bd4-6e8a-5215-b9ee-0ffa2a972515"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20030bd4-6e8a-5215-b9ee-0ffa2a972515" :answer-field/id #uuid "20030bd4-6e8a-5215-b9ee-0ffa2a972515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90a84480-b5fe-5e99-a5ca-05d902ef19d8" "answer-3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" "answer-5afcdc04-e77f-5dcd-84c4-8d2f63b67107" "answer-2d3ea076-4d0b-50b5-af4f-ca0b0399c71f" "answer-3d3656be-5473-5356-8bab-68c6041eb7db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90a84480-b5fe-5e99-a5ca-05d902ef19d8" :answer/id #uuid "90a84480-b5fe-5e99-a5ca-05d902ef19d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" :answer/id #uuid "3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5afcdc04-e77f-5dcd-84c4-8d2f63b67107" :answer/id #uuid "5afcdc04-e77f-5dcd-84c4-8d2f63b67107" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d3ea076-4d0b-50b5-af4f-ca0b0399c71f" :answer/id #uuid "2d3ea076-4d0b-50b5-af4f-ca0b0399c71f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d3656be-5473-5356-8bab-68c6041eb7db" :answer/id #uuid "3d3656be-5473-5356-8bab-68c6041eb7db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-36c5d3a3-e17d-52d4-a3d2-5e187ff26b13" "question-493d75d3-43a5-5a86-a486-dc06c6727b87" "question-e90c4b66-398a-540c-92b6-fce26b283a8e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3990de5-dc6f-5bc7-bcf1-3490082ecdc9" "question-10fc12bd-51f2-5325-ac89-b383736a7b6d" "question-047c28c1-e17d-56d3-9e40-ea3f7e7b2867"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-39855b88-dc8a-571a-b435-84e158ee3c93" "question-5f6f1f55-e6a2-5e50-9cd8-7d4ea9cc100b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-36c5d3a3-e17d-52d4-a3d2-5e187ff26b13" :question/id #uuid "36c5d3a3-e17d-52d4-a3d2-5e187ff26b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-336320" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Earth and Mars is $(7.83\\times {10}^{10})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Earth and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Earth and Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(7.83\\times {10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 6.42}{(7.83)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.169\\,76…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Earth and Mars is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{16}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93780ef7-4739-5931-a952-6ca27bf8e53f" "answer-field-405034c0-e463-548e-91c7-9b989518ba36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93780ef7-4739-5931-a952-6ca27bf8e53f" :answer-field/id #uuid "93780ef7-4739-5931-a952-6ca27bf8e53f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238" :answer/id #uuid "5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-405034c0-e463-548e-91c7-9b989518ba36" :answer-field/id #uuid "405034c0-e463-548e-91c7-9b989518ba36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827" :answer/id #uuid "4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311446 :answer-field/correct "answer-d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da" :answer/id #uuid "d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-493d75d3-43a5-5a86-a486-dc06c6727b87" :question/id #uuid "493d75d3-43a5-5a86-a486-dc06c6727b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-336274" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{7})}{(5.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.0\\cdot 2.5}{(5.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{4}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =5.336\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,5.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0a094d86-566e-5616-90d3-312561c336e3" "answer-field-e8b24067-f902-54cb-bce7-31c80a008ace"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0a094d86-566e-5616-90d3-312561c336e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a094d86-566e-5616-90d3-312561c336e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" :answer/id #uuid "1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8b24067-f902-54cb-bce7-31c80a008ace" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b24067-f902-54cb-bce7-31c80a008ace" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60" :answer/id #uuid "7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e90c4b66-398a-540c-92b6-fce26b283a8e" :question/id #uuid "e90c4b66-398a-540c-92b6-fce26b283a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336279" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Jupiter is $(7.78\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{(7.78\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.90}{(7.78)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(0.416\\,651…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(4.166\\,51…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2e108d7-70bb-59b2-b1bd-5f0398863020" "answer-field-056edfa7-0fe2-5df2-b784-93349b9866c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2e108d7-70bb-59b2-b1bd-5f0398863020" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e108d7-70bb-59b2-b1bd-5f0398863020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" :answer/id #uuid "0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-056edfa7-0fe2-5df2-b784-93349b9866c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "056edfa7-0fe2-5df2-b784-93349b9866c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" :answer/id #uuid "0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217786 :question/problem "The center of two objects of masses $(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.0\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(4.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.0\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{6})}{(4.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 7.5\\cdot 2.0}{(4.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =6.253\\,125\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,6.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216585 :answer-field/correct "answer-4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b" :answer/id #uuid "4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216586 :answer-field/correct "answer-2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" :answer/id #uuid "2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3990de5-dc6f-5bc7-bcf1-3490082ecdc9" :question/id #uuid "e3990de5-dc6f-5bc7-bcf1-3490082ecdc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336289" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(4.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(4.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.5}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-6}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.158…)\\times {10}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,2.2\\times {10}^{6}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d4ed601-5f68-5199-815d-6a11c05969c8" "answer-field-30b84e28-42b9-5c46-b2ac-bd1eafb073ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d4ed601-5f68-5199-815d-6a11c05969c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4ed601-5f68-5199-815d-6a11c05969c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a" :answer/id #uuid "e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30b84e28-42b9-5c46-b2ac-bd1eafb073ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "30b84e28-42b9-5c46-b2ac-bd1eafb073ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d" :answer/id #uuid "721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-10fc12bd-51f2-5325-ac89-b383736a7b6d" :question/id #uuid "10fc12bd-51f2-5325-ac89-b383736a7b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336292" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Earth is $(1.50\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Earth? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Earth is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Earth.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\cdot (1.50\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.52\\cdot (1.50)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{22}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.596\\,68…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(5.9668…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Earth is\n$(\\,5.97\\times {10}^{24}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04814265-845a-5b0c-b0e7-4e78d8ce847c" "answer-field-55511cd4-782c-5aba-a839-f537625df779"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04814265-845a-5b0c-b0e7-4e78d8ce847c" :answer-field/id #uuid "04814265-845a-5b0c-b0e7-4e78d8ce847c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133" :answer/id #uuid "d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.97" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55511cd4-782c-5aba-a839-f537625df779" :answer-field/id #uuid "55511cd4-782c-5aba-a839-f537625df779" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51" :answer/id #uuid "9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311089 :answer-field/correct "answer-81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f" :answer/id #uuid "81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217789 :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\cdot (3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.98\\cdot (3.84)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{20}\\cdot ({10}^{8})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(0.733\\,2…)\\times {10}^{23} \\\\  & =(7.332…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of the Moon is\n$(\\,7.33\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216591 :answer-field/correct "answer-ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1" :answer/id #uuid "ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216592 :answer-field/correct "answer-2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4" :answer/id #uuid "2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{22}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-047c28c1-e17d-56d3-9e40-ea3f7e7b2867" :question/id #uuid "047c28c1-e17d-56d3-9e40-ea3f7e7b2867" :question/math-academy-id "q-336317" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of Earth and Jupiter is $(6.29\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\cdot (6.29\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.91\\cdot (6.29)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{18}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(1.8977…)\\times {10}^{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Jupiter is\n$(\\,1.90\\times {10}^{27}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-319135a4-a585-5cc5-a698-7e9d448442eb" "answer-field-4420d3e9-6356-5714-a483-a1b091a3e136"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-319135a4-a585-5cc5-a698-7e9d448442eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "319135a4-a585-5cc5-a698-7e9d448442eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18" :answer/id #uuid "33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4420d3e9-6356-5714-a483-a1b091a3e136" :answer-field/id #uuid "4420d3e9-6356-5714-a483-a1b091a3e136" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50" :answer/id #uuid "5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311321 :answer-field/correct "answer-d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7" :answer/id #uuid "d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217790 :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.9\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (9.5\\times {10}^{5})}{1.9\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.2\\cdot 9.5}{1.9}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =206.77\\times {10}^{3} \\\\  & =20.677\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.677\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.677}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.547…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.5\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216593 :answer-field/correct "answer-e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73" :answer/id #uuid "e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216594 :answer-field/correct "answer-9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606" :answer/id #uuid "9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186310961 :answer-field/correct "answer-e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1" :answer/id #uuid "e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-39855b88-dc8a-571a-b435-84e158ee3c93" :question/id #uuid "39855b88-dc8a-571a-b435-84e158ee3c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-336310" :question/problem "Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(2.69\\times {10}^{17})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Mars and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Mars and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{2.69\\times {10}^{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.42\\cdot 1.90}{2.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{23}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{{10}^{17}} \\\\  & =(30.245…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{30.245…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30.245…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(5.499…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(\\,5.50\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65c170d4-db55-5f82-97f4-a86ba579f024" "answer-field-fdfc0d2a-f06a-5c20-bd50-0265e3825c1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65c170d4-db55-5f82-97f4-a86ba579f024" :answer-field/id #uuid "65c170d4-db55-5f82-97f4-a86ba579f024" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b0a01ef6-9c9b-5d09-92b6-aaa9a3b3eb9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0a01ef6-9c9b-5d09-92b6-aaa9a3b3eb9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answe